  {
    date: 'Oct 8, 2026',
    iso: '2026-10-08',
    material: true,
    summary: 'The previous PostHog project, holding all analytics recorded before October 8, 2026, was deleted. Analytics have gone to a new project since 00:00 UTC on October 8. Also: two ways typed text could reach analytics are closed, shorter page titles and descriptions for search results, a menu on phones, and a new learning path page with one page per level, an optional helper for choosing a level, and a search that keeps your words out of the page address.',
    details: [
      'Deleted: every analytics event recorded before October 8, 2026, including the pageview and page-leave events that carried what people typed into the loan comparison and the net worth calculator (see October 7). We deleted the whole PostHog project rather than searching for the affected events one by one, so the anonymous visitor profiles, click data and heatmaps from that period are gone too. None of it can be restored.',
      'Since 00:00 UTC on October 8, 2026, analytics go to a new PostHog Cloud EU project; nothing was recorded between the pause on October 7 and then. What is collected, when, and under what consent is unchanged, as described above. The newsletter and waitlist server-side events go to the same new project.',
      'Search results: lessons whose title ran past about 60 characters in search results now give search engines and link previews a shorter title (for example "Introduction to Financial Independence | nidhi"); the heading on the page is unchanged. The homepage and three lessons have shorter descriptions, and the anti-bias lesson\'s description now explains what many people do instead of telling you what to do. Wording only; nothing is collected or sent.',
      'Phone menu: on phones, the site\'s links move into a menu in the header. New data-attr labels nav-menu, nav-menu-contact and one per link (nav-menu-blog, nav-menu-about, nav-menu-email and so on). As with every click, they are recorded only if you accept the cookie banner.',
      'Learning path search: the words you search for go into the page address (?q=), and pageview events carry the address, so they reached our analytics. The analytics script now removes ?q= from learning path addresses before any event is sent.',
      'Click text: if you accepted the cookie banner, a recorded click, including a click on something that does not respond, carried the text of what was clicked. On the free tools that text can be a vendor name you typed or a figure worked out from your numbers. Clicks are now recorded without any text, told apart by their data-attr labels only.',
      'Learning path (/blog/): the page no longer lists every lesson. It shows where to start, or, if you have read lessons, the next one; the five levels and Inclusive Finances as one list, each with how many lessons it has (and how many you have read); and a search box. Each level\'s lessons are on their own page (/blog/discovery/, /blog/building/ and so on), with the same read toggles as before. Starting points, counts and "You are here" come from the existing nidhi-reading-progress key in your browser. Nothing is sent to us or anyone else, and no new key is stored.',
      'Search: what you type into the learning path\'s search box stays on the page. It is no longer written into the page address (?q=), so it never reaches page-view analytics. When a search engine sends you to /blog/?q=..., the page uses those words for the search and then removes them from the address. The analytics script still strips ?q= from learning path addresses as a backstop, and the search on topic pages (/blog/tag/...) is unchanged.',
      'Level helper: "Help me choose a level" asks which ideas you could explain to a friend and what would help most right now, then suggests a level to begin with and the lessons on the ideas you did not tick. Your answers are kept only while the page is open: they are not stored in your browser, put in the address or sent anywhere. Its area is excluded from recorded clicks, and while it is open click heatmaps are paused, so that, even if you accepted the cookie banner, nothing records which answer you picked. Following its link to a level is an ordinary page view of that level\'s page.',
      'New data-attr labels, recorded only if you accept the cookie banner, as before: blog-start-first-lesson, blog-start-continue, blog-start-inclusive, blog-level-helper (opening or closing the helper, not its answers), blog-level-discovery, blog-level-building, blog-level-psychology, blog-level-optimizing and blog-level-mastery, blog-search-result and blog-search-show-all (never the words searched), and on the level pages level-page-crumb, level-page-covered-<level>, level-page-prev, level-page-next, level-page-all, level-page-inclusive and level-page-any-country, and blog-index-any-country for the note that lessons are written for any country. In the header, Learn now opens a menu like Free Tools: nav-learn (the menu button), nav-blog (Learning path, the same destination as before), nav-blog-topics and nav-learn-editorial (How lessons are written, the editorial policy), with nav-menu-learn-editorial for the same link in the phone menu. The existing blog-index-browse-topics, blog-index-inclusive-hub, blog-card-*, lp-mark-level-read-* and lp-mark-level-unread-* labels keep their meaning. Lesson pages and the homepage now link to the level pages instead of a section of /blog/; their labels are unchanged.',
    ],
  },
  {
    date: 'Oct 7, 2026',
    iso: '2026-10-07',
    material: true,
    summary: 'Fixed free tools that put what you typed into page addresses analytics recorded. Also: a homepage that leads with learning, starting routes you can follow, lessons that show where you are and end with what you now understand, a simpler one-currency net worth view, Frankfurter named in the notice, and OpenAI\'s training crawler blocked.',
    details: [
      'Internal visits: the team\'s own browsers can be marked so they are never counted. Opening any page with ?internal=1 sets a new key in your browser, nidhi-internal, and from then on that browser runs no analytics at all, not even anonymous pageviews; ?internal=0 removes it. The parameter is taken out of the address right away. Visitors who never open such a link see no change.',
      'Cookie settings: a "Cookie settings" link at the bottom of every page reopens the cookie banner, so you can change your answer as easily as you gave it. Before, a choice could only be undone by clearing your browser\'s site data. Choosing "No thanks" there after an earlier yes switches click recording off immediately, not just from your next visit. The choice is still stored only in your browser (nidhi-cookie-consent), and the link\'s own click is recorded only if you had accepted (data-attr footer-cookie-settings).',
      'Analytics paused, then a fresh start: from today until 00:00 UTC on October 8, 2026, the site records no analytics at all, and the newsletter sends no events either. From then on, analytics go to a new, empty PostHog Cloud EU project, under the same rules described above. The previous PostHog project, holding everything recorded before today, is being deleted.',
      'Free tools, what went wrong: the loan comparison (since it launched on May 10, 2026) and the net worth calculator (since May 17, 2026) wrote what you typed into the page address as you typed, so the page could be reloaded or bookmarked. Every page sends an anonymous pageview and page-leave event, and those carry the page address. So the amounts, rates, names and currencies you entered on those two tools reached our analytics (PostHog Cloud EU) inside that address, and so did the contents of any shared link opened to one of them. A link to any of the three tools with values after a ? would have been recorded the same way. The events carried no name, email or user ID, but this broke our promise that the values you type are never sent.',
      'Beliefs and the learning path: the principle behind every lesson is now said where people meet the site. The beliefs page has a new belief, "Not just the rules of thumb": a rule of thumb is a starting point, and each lesson explains why it exists, where it breaks and what depends on you. The learning path introduction says the same in one sentence. Wording only.',
      'Footer disclaimer: "nidhi offers guidance" and "Our suggestions are educational" read like advice, which the rest of the site is careful not to give. It now says nidhi provides educational information and tools, and that the content and tools are educational, not professional financial, investment or tax advice. Wording only.',
      'Homepage FAQ, "What happens to the numbers I type into the tools?": it said the numbers are not sent "to nidhi or anyone else". That was broader than precise. It now says they stay on your device and are not sent to nidhi, never go into the page address, and that when a tool needs outside data such as exchange rates, your browser asks that service for the rates only, never your amounts, which like any request shows it your IP address. Nothing changed in what is sent.',
      'Phones: no page scrolls sideways any more. Wide lesson tables scroll inside their own box (a small script wraps each table in a labelled, keyboard-focusable region as the page loads; it reads and stores nothing), the loan comparison\'s screen-reader data tables no longer widen the page, lesson text has the same side margin as its title, breadcrumbs on the tools and topics pages are at least 24 pixels tall, and topic card lesson counts are at least 12 pixels. Spaces that went missing next to links on the about, editorial policy, free tools, privacy and subscription-confirmed pages are back. Layout and wording only.',
      'Net worth calculator on phones: in the Share window, the Cancel and Copy link buttons sat half under the recipient preview, so a tap on their lower half missed. They now sit above it. Layout only.',
      'Free tools, what changed: no tool writes your inputs into the page address any more. A share link carries them after the # sign, a part of the address browsers never send to a server, and the page moves them out of the address bar before analytics start, so the pageview never sees them. Older links that carried the values after a ? still open, and are cleaned the same way. As a backstop, the analytics script now removes everything except campaign parameters (utm_source and the like) from any free-tool address before an event is sent. The section on what we collect now says this. One consequence: reloading a tool page no longer keeps what you typed; a share link keeps a copy. The events already recorded with these addresses are being deleted from PostHog.',
      'Lessons: every lesson and Inclusive Finances guide now ends with a short "What you now understand" list restating its main ideas, in a tinted box that mirrors the TL;DR at the top, before the free tool and the previous and next links.',
      'Homepage, for readers who already know the basics: a line under the hero buttons links straight to the Building and Psychology levels and to the free tools (data-attr labels home-hero-skip-building, home-hero-skip-psychology and home-hero-skip-tools, captured only with consent, as before), each starting card says who its route suits, and a short section explains how lessons go past the rule of thumb. No change to what data is collected.',
      'Lessons: each lesson in Discovery, Building, Psychology, Optimizing and Mastery says where it sits in its level (for example "Lesson 8 of 16") at the top and above the previous and next links, with a link to that level on the learning path. That link is at least 24 pixels tall, the minimum size for a tap target. The numbers come from the published lessons when the site is built; nothing about your reading is used.',
      'Lessons: those with a paired free tool end in a short path: the lesson you read, the idea in one line, and a link to the tool. Two more lessons now link to the net worth calculator: What Is Net Worth and Managing Money Across Currencies.',
      'Homepage, "Start wherever you are": each situation shows its lessons as a numbered route, names the free tools that go with it, and ends in one button into the route. If you have read lessons on that route, the button moves to the first one you have not read, and once you have read them all it points to the learning path. It reads the existing nidhi-reading-progress key in your browser to do this. Nothing is sent to us or anyone else, and no new key is stored.',
      'Homepage: numbered lessons keep their titles beside their numbers, "nidhi grows with your financial life" has more space between stages, and on phones the example chart is drawn narrower so its labels stay readable. Its footnote, and the description of the chart for screen readers, are now printed once in the page, not once per drawing. Small labels are at least 12 pixels, and the "Free Tools" menu button in the header is at least 24 pixels tall, the minimum size for a tap target.',
      'Homepage redesign: the page now leads with the free lessons and tools: where to start for your situation, which lesson goes with which tool, and how nidhi keeps up as finances get more complex. The planner and its early-access list come after them. The early-access form in the hero is gone; the one further down is the same form, sending and storing exactly what it did before. A short FAQ on the homepage is also published as structured data for search engines.',
      'Homepage example: the look-ahead step now grows an invested balance (€3,400 invested, €150 added a month, an illustrative 5% a year after inflation) instead of the whole net worth, because a net worth made of a car, cash, a pension and a loan does not grow at one rate. Its note now counts the starting €3,400 as money put in alongside the €18,000 added. Editorial only; nothing is collected or sent.',
      'Homepage: if you have read lessons on the learning path, it suggests the next one, using the same nidhi-reading-progress key. Without any reading progress it suggests the first lesson.',
      'Homepage: the multi-currency example no longer contacts Frankfurter (the European Central Bank exchange-rate service) when the page loads, and the page no longer opens a connection to it in advance. The request is made only if you press "Add it up in euros at today\'s rates", and it carries none of the amounts on the page or anything you typed. Like any request your browser makes, it shows Frankfurter your IP address and browser type.',
      'Frankfurter in the notice: until now it was mentioned only in changelog entries. "Where it lives" now has a paragraph on it: when your browser contacts it, what the request contains, and what it reveals. Earlier entries said the request carries "no personal data". That was imprecise: it carries only currency codes, never your amounts, but like any request your browser makes it shows Frankfurter your IP address and browser type, and an IP address can count as personal data. The earlier entries stay as written, as a record; this corrects them. Nothing changed in what the site sends.',
      'New data-attr labels, so consented interaction analytics can tell links apart: on the homepage home-hero-start-learning, home-hero-tools, home-hero-first-lesson, home-hero-continue, home-start-* (including the route buttons home-start-basics-door, home-start-no-plan-door, home-start-habits-door and home-start-complex-door, and the route tool links home-start-<route>-tool-1 and -tool-2), home-pair-lesson-*, home-tools-all, home-grows-*, home-mc-convert, home-mc-tool, home-editorial-policy, home-privacy and home-faq-*; on lessons post-series-top and post-series-bottom. Without consent, these clicks are not captured, as before; only the anonymous pageviews described above are recorded.',
      'Net worth calculator: when everything you enter is in your own currency, the results show what you own, what you owe and your net worth, instead of a currency chart and per-currency risk cards. This view needs no exchange rates. Add something in another currency and the currency chart and risk cards appear as before. Wording now says "your currency" instead of "functional currency". Everything still runs in your browser, and nothing new is sent or stored.',
      'Inclusive Finances, once its first guide is published: a full-width card in "Start wherever you are" on the homepage, listing the situations the guides cover and linking to all of them, and a card beside the numbered levels on the learning path, using the existing data-attr labels home-inclusive-finances and blog-index-inclusive-hub. The hub joins the sitemap, the list of pages we offer to search engines, in the same update, dated by its most recently published or updated guide.',
      'Names: the navigation link and breadcrumbs say "Learn" and the footer link says "Learning path". They still lead to /blog/ and keep their nav-blog and footer-blog labels. The learning path page is titled "Learn Personal Finance", and the subscription pages, the topic list, the free tools page, the about page and the beliefs page call them lessons or the learning path instead of "the blog". The newsletter itself, and what it collects, are unchanged. The net worth tool is now called the net worth calculator; its address (/free/multi-currency-net-worth/) and link labels are unchanged.',
      'The trust section on the homepage says nidhi is built for different households, countries, abilities and ways of working, and names what that means in practice: the site works with screen readers, its charts do not rely on colour alone, it follows your device\'s reduced motion setting, and every lesson opens with a short summary. The beliefs page names the needs it means instead of saying "wired differently".',
      'Crawlers and AI tools: robots.txt, the file that tells crawlers which pages they may read, now blocks GPTBot, the crawler OpenAI uses to gather training data. OAI-SearchBot, which powers ChatGPT search and links back to our pages, stays allowed. llms.txt, the plain-text summary of the site for AI tools, calls the lessons the learning path and opens with who nidhi is for.',
      'The short social videos made from lessons no longer mention a free tool before that tool is available.',
      'Social posts made from the upcoming Optimizing and Inclusive Finances lessons now follow the same "teach, don\'t prescribe" wording as the lessons: they describe what many people do and what depends on your situation instead of telling you what to do, carry their sources and assumptions, and drop claims stronger than the lessons make. The one-line teasers these posts use to point back to each lesson were updated to match, and one lesson figure was corrected (€500 a month at 5% a year after inflation for 30 years is about €408,000, using the same method as the lesson\'s other figures). The upcoming cash management lesson\'s FAQ no longer says rate-sensitive funds are fine for money needed within a year: their price drop when rates rise is immediate, as the lesson itself explains. Editorial only.',
      'Editorial pass over the 42 published lessons: claims stated more firmly than the evidence supports now say where the rule of thumb breaks, figures that disagreed between lessons now agree (long-run stock returns of about 4 to 6% a year after inflation, one threshold for high-interest debt, one order for emergency fund, debt and employer match), two figures were corrected against their sources (long-run bond returns, and how much more often investors sell winners than losers), and one attribution was fixed (what Robert Shiller\'s Nobel Prize recognised). The 36 lessons this changed now show today as their update date. Editorial only.',
      'Second editorial pass, "teach, don\'t prescribe": across all lessons, published and upcoming, wording that told readers what to do with their money (what to buy, which debt to pay first, which account to open) now explains what people commonly do, why, the evidence, where it breaks and what depends on your situation. Upcoming Optimizing and Inclusive Finances lessons were brought in line with the published figures, and a few errors were fixed along the way (a retirement formula, an example\'s arithmetic, and a statement that money-market funds are deposit-insured). Published lessons this changed show today as their update date. Editorial only.',
      'This changelog is easier to read: the latest entry and the two before it are shown in full, and everything older sits under one collapsed "Earlier changes" section, grouped by month. Every entry is kept word for word; nothing was removed.',
      'This changelog now keeps one entry per day. Days that had several entries (May 25, October 1, October 5 and today) were combined into one each. Only repeated lines were dropped; no change was left out.',
    ],
  },
  {
    date: 'Oct 5, 2026',
    iso: '2026-10-05',
    material: true,
    summary: 'Fixed two kinds of analytics that could run without your consent and a cookie banner that could stick open, plus interactive charts in both tools, a continuous homepage, a two-column learning path, and tool links inside articles.',
    details: [
      'Analytics, what went wrong: two PostHog features, page-speed measurements ("web vitals") and clicks on things that did not respond ("dead clicks"), were switched on in our PostHog project settings. Our site code did not switch them off for visitors who had not accepted the cookie banner, so they ran for those visitors too. In the 30 days before the fix that was 59 page-speed events and 8 dead-click events across 23 visits. They carried no name, email or user ID, but they went beyond the anonymous pageviews this policy promises without consent.',
      'Analytics, what changed: the site now sets every PostHog capture feature itself instead of leaving any to the project settings. Dead clicks are recorded only after you accept, together with heatmaps and autocaptured clicks. Page-speed measurements and browser error capture are off for everyone. The "Only if you click Sure, that\'s fine" list above now names dead clicks explicitly and says page-speed measurements and browser errors are not recorded.',
      'Cookie banner: if you clicked "Sure, that\'s fine" before the analytics script finished loading, or with an ad blocker that stops it, the banner could stay open. It now always closes, and your choice is applied as soon as the script loads, or never if it is blocked.',
      'Multi-currency net worth: pointing at, tapping or selecting a currency in the chart or its legend highlights it and shows its share and amount. The legend lists amounts as well as shares. Shared links that hide amounts still hide them. Percentages follow your spending currency\'s number format, and a currency with no exchange rate is left out of the chart and named underneath, instead of being drawn at a size that mixed two currencies.',
      'Loan comparison: the balance-over-time chart has a years axis, more gridlines, and lines told apart by dash pattern as well as colour. Pointing at it, tapping it or using the arrow keys shows each loan\'s remaining balance and the interest paid so far, all in your browser. The payment-split chart shows whole amounts in your currency\'s usual format.',
      'Homepage: it no longer snaps between full-screen sections and scrolls as one page. The examples in the hero are calculated from the assumptions printed under each chart, and you can point at or tap a chart to read its values. The multi-currency example converts its amounts at the day\'s European Central Bank rates in your browser, through the same Frankfurter service the page already used; the request now asks for the US dollar and Indian rupee rates instead of only the dollar. The free tools and reading links appear as cards with small previews.',
      'Learning path: on wide screens each level\'s posts sit in two columns, numbered in reading order, with a note to read left to right, then down. Phones keep one column. The subscribe box puts the email field and button on one row, with the RSS link and the dismiss links beneath; nothing changes in what the form sends or stores. Removed the line promising new posts every Monday, Wednesday and Friday, which no longer matched the publishing schedule.',
      'Some posts mention their paired free tool once inside the article, at the point where it helps, as well as in the "Free tool" card after it. The mention is a plain link to the tool page with its own data-attr label (post-inline-tool-loan-comparison or post-inline-tool-multi-currency-net-worth), so consented interaction analytics can tell the two placements apart. Without consent, nothing is captured, as before.',
    ],
